cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "CoRTeC: A Differentially Private Release for Synthetic Tabular Data, Decodable by Any Generator"
message: "If you use this software or the accompanying paper, please cite it."
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
version: "1.2.5"
date-released: "2026-10-30"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22945315"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Calyie/cortec"
abstract: >-
  The records that could train the most useful models, such as a hospital's patient files, are
  often the ones an institution cannot share. We propose CoRTeC, a method that releases synthetic
  versions of such records under differential privacy. The synthetic data exposes no personally
  identifiable information (PII), and differential privacy places a proven limit on membership
  inference. CoRTeC spends the privacy budget once, on a small file of noisy counts designed for
  training a classifier. For each group of records, the file gives the group's size, its balance
  of outcomes, a histogram of every column for each outcome and the outcome rate in each of
  several non-overlapping cells. A reviewer can check every number before the file leaves the
  institution. Anyone can then turn the counts into synthetic rows, as often as they like and at
  no further privacy cost. A maximum-entropy decoder can do this without any language model, and
  so can each of the five frozen language models we tested. None of them ever sees a real record.
  A selection step then matches the rows to the released counts. Decoded without a language model,
  the automatically configured release at ε = 2 trains classifiers to within 0.02 AUC of a real
  sample of the same size on the Adult dataset, across three data splits. The gap is larger on
  NHANES and bank marketing, reaching 0.07 AUC on the medical data. Because the counts are fixed
  in advance, the release reproduces the outcome rates a deployment asks for, at some cost in
  accuracy on pairs of columns. Four controls show that most of the utility comes from the release
  and the selection step, and that a language model adds what it knows from the column names. No
  private row ever reaches a model, and no synthetic row copies a real one. The strongest
  membership attack stays far below the bound the budget allows. This repository holds the
  Apache-2.0 reference implementation with its tests, the research pipeline, the paper, the
  deployment guide and the frozen auto-configurations.
